# 10. So now we have four different "intelligence" layers

This is probably the cleanest way to think about your whole project:

| Layer                      | Question it answers                           | Technology                              |
| -------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------- |
| **Measurement**            | *How much energy is actually being consumed?* | RAPL / NVML / CodeCarbon / profiler     |
| **Detection**              | *Where is the energy being consumed?*         | Profiling + instrumentation             |
| **Diagnosis**              | *Why is this code expensive?*                 | AST + runtime analysis                  |
| **Optimization knowledge** | *What transformation is appropriate?*         | Research-backed optimization repository |
| **Generation**             | *How do we implement that transformation?*    | LLM                                     |
| **Validation**             | *Did it actually work?*                       | Tests + real energy measurement         |

And this resolves the problem with the XGBoost idea.

### XGBoost is optional.

It is **not the heart of OASIS**.

You don't need a model that says:

> "This code = 8.7 J."

if you already have the ability to actually measure energy and identify hotspots.

A learned predictor could later be used to reduce profiling overhead, but it should **not be the core of your system**.

