# 12. The fine-grained issue → technique map

This is the table I would use as the starting taxonomy for OASIS—not as the final KB yet.

| Energy issue                     | How to detect                                | Static/Dynamic | Candidate optimization                  | What must be proven                           | Main tools             |
| -------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- | -------------- | --------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- | ---------------------- |
| **Repeated computation**         | Same computation repeated with same inputs   | Both           | Memoization/caching                     | Result equivalence + energy decrease          | Tree-sitter + profiler |
| **Nested loops**                 | Loop nesting + high execution count          | Both           | Algorithmic restructuring               | Lower work without behavior change            | Tree-sitter + profiler |
| **High algorithmic complexity**  | Complexity + runtime growth                  | Both           | More efficient algorithm/data structure | Reduced operations/energy                     | AST + benchmarks       |
| **Repeated linear lookup**       | Membership/search inside hot loop            | Static         | Hash/set/index                          | Same semantics + lower lookup cost            | AST                    |
| **Repeated DB queries**          | Query execution inside loop                  | Dynamic        | Batching/query restructuring            | Same DB result + lower DB activity            | DB profiler            |
| **Repeated file I/O**            | Frequent read/write calls                    | Dynamic        | Buffering/batching                      | Same output + fewer I/O operations            | I/O profiler           |
| **Unnecessary computation**      | Computation whose result is unused/redundant | Static         | Remove computation                      | Program behavior unchanged                    | AST                    |
| **Repeated object allocation**   | High allocation count in hotspot             | Dynamic        | Object reuse                            | Same behavior + lower allocation              | Runtime profiler       |
| **Excessive garbage collection** | GC frequency/time                            | Dynamic        | Reduce allocations/object lifetime      | Same behavior + lower GC                      | Runtime profiler       |
| **Unnecessary data copying**     | Copy operations in hot path                  | Both           | Views/references/in-place operations    | No aliasing/semantic changes                  | AST + profiler         |
| **Poor memory locality**         | Cache/memory-access behavior                 | Dynamic        | Data-layout restructuring               | Same result + lower memory traffic            | Hardware profiler      |
| **Excessive serialization**      | Frequent encode/decode operations            | Dynamic        | Reduce/batch serialization              | Same communication semantics                  | Profiler               |
| **Excessive network calls**      | Many small requests                          | Dynamic        | Request batching                        | Same response semantics                       | Network profiler       |
| **Polling**                      | Repeated checks with little useful work      | Dynamic        | Event-driven/wait strategy              | Same responsiveness/behavior                  | Runtime tracing        |
| **Synchronization overhead**     | Lock/wait contention                         | Dynamic        | Reduce synchronization                  | Thread safety preserved                       | Concurrency profiler   |
| **Poor parallelism**             | Independent work executed sequentially       | Both           | Parallel execution                      | Energy **must be measured**; faster ≠ greener | Profiler               |
| **Excessive parallelism**        | Too many threads/processes                   | Dynamic        | Reduce concurrency                      | Same throughput + lower energy                | Runtime profiler       |
| **CPU-intensive operation**      | CPU hotspot                                  | Dynamic        | Algorithm/vectorization/etc.            | Actual energy reduction                       | CPU profiler           |
| **GPU underutilization**         | Low GPU utilization                          | Dynamic        | Batching/vectorization                  | Actual energy reduction                       | NVML/profiler          |
| **CPU↔GPU transfers**            | Frequent host/device transfers               | Dynamic        | Reduce/batch transfers                  | Same computation + lower transfer overhead    | GPU profiler           |
| **Large memory footprint**       | High memory consumption                      | Dynamic        | Data representation/reuse               | Same functionality + lower memory energy      | Memory profiler        |

### Notice something important:

**"Parallelize it" is not automatically an energy optimization.**

Neither is:

**"make it faster."**

That is exactly why your final validation must measure **energy**, not just runtime.

A transformation can make something faster while consuming **more instantaneous power**, or consume more total energy under some workload/hardware conditions.

