## And I think this is the point where your project becomes manageable

You don't need to solve:

> **"What programming technique is energy efficient?"**

for all software.

You need to build a system that does:

```text
                  CODEBASE
                     │
                     ▼
              Runtime profiling
                     │
                     ▼
             Energy measurement
                     │
                     ▼
             ENERGY HOTSPOT
                     │
                     ▼
             ISSUE DIAGNOSIS
                     │
                     ▼
       Research-backed optimization
                     │
                     ▼
                User approval
                     │
                     ▼
                    LLM
                     │
                     ▼
              Generated patch
                     │
                     ▼
             Functional testing
                     │
              ┌──────┴──────┐
             FAIL           PASS
              │              │
            Reject           ▼
                       Energy testing
                              │
                    ┌─────────┴─────────┐
                  Worse                 Better
                    │                     │
                  Revert                Apply
```

**That is OASIS.**

And the part we should tackle next is **not coding the LLM**. The hardest research-critical part is actually the middle:

> **Given an energy hotspot, how do we reliably classify the underlying issue and map it to an optimization strategy using existing research evidence?**

That is where we should design the taxonomy and evidence methodology carefully, because that determines whether OASIS is a real research system or just an LLM making guesses.
